// The tool, the catalogue it defines, and the fixtures that are read as if they
// were target projects.
//
// This file is committed on purpose, against the usual advice, because without
// it a clone builds the tool against the *published* spec rather than the one
// beside it. The alternative — a replace in go.mod — is not available: `go
// install .../cmd/speclink@version` refuses a module that carries one, and that
// is the only way anybody gets this tool. A workspace is invisible to go
// install, so it is the one mechanism that serves both.
//
// The fixtures are listed for a blunt reason: a workspace refuses to build a
// module nested inside it that it does not know about, and every one of them is
// a module because that is what a target project is. Their own replace
// directives are still what the tests exercise, because a fixture is copied out
// of the tree before it is verified, and a copy is outside this workspace.
//
// One command does not cover everything — a module is its own package pattern
// either way:
//
//	go test ./... ./spec/...
go 1.27.0

use (
	.
	./spec
	./testdata/arch
	./testdata/bad
	./testdata/bare
	./testdata/example
)
